            new Blog(
                new LocalizedText("Understanding Entity Framework Core Performance", "Comprendre les performances d'Entity Framework Core"),
                new LocalizedText("Tips and tricks to optimize your EF Core queries for better performance and scalability.", 
                                 "Conseils et astuces pour optimiser vos requêtes EF Core pour de meilleures performances et évolutivité."),
                new LocalizedText("# Understanding Entity Framework Core Performance\n\nEntity Framework Core is a powerful ORM, but poor performance can be a significant issue if not handled properly. This guide covers essential performance optimization techniques.\n\n## Common Performance Issues\n\n### N+1 Query Problem\n\n```csharp\n// Bad - Triggers N+1 queries\nvar blogs = context.Blogs.ToList();\nforeach (var blog in blogs)\n{\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{blog.Title} by {blog.Author.Name}\");\n}\n\n// Good - Uses eager loading\nvar blogs = context.Blogs\n    .Include(b => b.Author)\n    .ToList();\n```\n\n### Inefficient Queries\n\n```csharp\n// Bad - Loads all data to memory\nvar recentBlogs = context.Blogs\n    .ToList()\n    .Where(b => b.PublishedAt > DateTime.UtcNow.AddDays(-30))\n    .Take(10);\n\n// Good - Filters at database level\nvar recentBlogs = context.Blogs\n    .Where(b => b.PublishedAt > DateTime.UtcNow.AddDays(-30))\n    .Take(10)\n    .ToList();\n```\n\n## Optimization Strategies\n\n### 1. Use Projections\n\n```csharp\n// Instead of loading entire entities\nvar blogTitles = context.Blogs\n    .Select(b => new { b.Title, b.PublishedAt })\n    .ToList();\n```\n\n### 2. Implement Pagination\n\n```csharp\npublic async Task<PagedResult<Blog>> GetBlogsAsync(int page, int pageSize)\n{\n    var query = context.Blogs\n        .Where(b => b.IsPublished)\n        .OrderByDescending(b => b.PublishedAt);\n    \n    var totalCount = await query.CountAsync();\n    var blogs = await query\n        .Skip((page - 1) * pageSize)\n        .Take(pageSize)\n        .ToListAsync();\n    \n    return new PagedResult<Blog>\n    {\n        Items = blogs,\n        TotalCount = totalCount,\n        Page = page,\n        PageSize = pageSize\n    };\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Use Bulk Operations\n\n```csharp\n// For large datasets, use EF Core Plus or similar\ncontext.Blogs\n    .Where(b => b.Views < 100)\n    .BatchUpdate(b => new Blog { IsArchived = true });\n```\n\n### 4. Optimize Database Queries\n\n```csharp\n// Use indexes\n[Index(nameof(PublishedAt))]\n[Index(nameof(CategoryId), nameof(PublishedAt))]\npublic class Blog\n{\n    public DateTime PublishedAt { get; set; }\n    public int CategoryId { get; set; }\n}\n```\n\n### 5. Use Compiled Queries\n\n```csharp\nprivate static readonly Func<BlogContext, int, IAsyncEnumerable<Blog>> GetBlogsByAuthor =\n    EF.CompileAsyncQuery((BlogContext context, int authorId) =>\n        context.Blogs.Where(b => b.AuthorId == authorId));\n\n// Usage\nvar blogs = await GetBlogsByAuthor(context, authorId).ToListAsync();\n```\n\n## Monitoring and Profiling\n\n### 1. Enable Query Logging\n\n```csharp\nprotected override void OnConfiguring(DbContextOptionsBuilder optionsBuilder)\n{\n    optionsBuilder\n        .UseSqlServer(connectionString)\n        .EnableSensitiveDataLogging()\n        .LogTo(Console.WriteLine, LogLevel.Information);\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Use Query Tags\n\n```csharp\nvar blogs = context.Blogs\n    .TagWith(\"GetRecentBlogs\")\n    .Where(b => b.PublishedAt > DateTime.UtcNow.AddDays(-30))\n    .ToList();\n```\n\n### 3. Track Query Performance\n\n```csharp\nvar stopwatch = Stopwatch.StartNew();\nvar blogs = await context.Blogs.ToListAsync();\nstopwatch.Stop();\n\n_logger.LogInformation(\"Query executed in {ElapsedMilliseconds}ms\", stopwatch.ElapsedMilliseconds);\n```\n\n## Best Practices\n\n1. **Use async/await** for all database operations\n2. **Avoid loading unnecessary data** with projections\n3. **Use appropriate loading strategies** (lazy, eager, explicit)\n4. **Implement proper caching** for frequently accessed data\n5. **Monitor query execution plans** regularly\n6. **Use connection pooling** effectively\n7. **Batch operations** when possible\n8. **Keep contexts short-lived** in web applications\n\n## Performance Testing\n\n```csharp\n[Fact]\npublic async Task GetRecentBlogs_ShouldExecuteInLessThan100ms()\n{\n    // Arrange\n    var stopwatch = Stopwatch.StartNew();\n    \n    // Act\n    var blogs = await _blogService.GetRecentBlogsAsync(10);\n    stopwatch.Stop();\n    \n    // Assert\n    Assert.True(stopwatch.ElapsedMilliseconds < 100);\n    Assert.Equal(10, blogs.Count);\n}\n```\n\nRemember: premature optimization is the root of all evil, but being aware of these patterns will help you write more efficient code from the start.",
                                 "# Comprendre les performances d'Entity Framework Core\n\nEntity Framework Core est un ORM puissant, mais de mauvaises performances peuvent être un problème significatif si elles ne sont pas gérées correctement. Ce guide couvre les techniques essentielles d'optimisation des performances.\n\n## Problèmes de performance courants\n\n### Problème de requête N+1\n\n```csharp\n// Mauvais - Déclenche N+1 requêtes\nvar blogs = context.Blogs.ToList();\nforeach (var blog in blogs)\n{\n    Console.WriteLine($\"{blog.Title} par {blog.Author.Name}\");\n}\n\n// Bon - Utilise le chargement eager\nvar blogs = context.Blogs\n    .Include(b => b.Author)\n    .ToList();\n```\n\n### Requêtes inefficaces\n\n```csharp\n// Mauvais - Charge toutes les données en mémoire\nvar recentBlogs = context.Blogs\n    .ToList()\n    .Where(b => b.PublishedAt > DateTime.UtcNow.AddDays(-30))\n    .Take(10);\n\n// Bon - Filtre au niveau de la base de données\nvar recentBlogs = context.Blogs\n    .Where(b => b.PublishedAt > DateTime.UtcNow.AddDays(-30))\n    .Take(10)\n    .ToList();\n```\n\n## Stratégies d'optimisation\n\n### 1. Utiliser les projections\n\n```csharp\n// Au lieu de charger des entités entières\nvar blogTitles = context.Blogs\n    .Select(b => new { b.Title, b.PublishedAt })\n    .ToList();\n```\n\n### 2. Implémenter la pagination\n\n```csharp\npublic async Task<PagedResult<Blog>> GetBlogsAsync(int page, int pageSize)\n{\n    var query = context.Blogs\n        .Where(b => b.IsPublished)\n        .OrderByDescending(b => b.PublishedAt);\n    \n    var totalCount = await query.CountAsync();\n    var blogs = await query\n        .Skip((page - 1) * pageSize)\n        .Take(pageSize)\n        .ToListAsync();\n    \n    return new PagedResult<Blog>\n    {\n        Items = blogs,\n        TotalCount = totalCount,\n        Page = page,\n        PageSize = pageSize\n    };\n}\n```\n\n### 3. Utiliser les opérations en lot\n\n```csharp\n// Pour les gros jeux de données, utilisez EF Core Plus ou similaire\ncontext.Blogs\n    .Where(b => b.Views < 100)\n    .BatchUpdate(b => new Blog { IsArchived = true });\n```\n\n### 4. Optimiser les requêtes de base de données\n\n```csharp\n// Utilisez les index\n[Index(nameof(PublishedAt))]\n[Index(nameof(CategoryId), nameof(PublishedAt))]\npublic class Blog\n{\n    public DateTime PublishedAt { get; set; }\n    public int CategoryId { get; set; }\n}\n```\n\n### 5. Utiliser les requêtes compilées\n\n```csharp\nprivate static readonly Func<BlogContext, int, IAsyncEnumerable<Blog>> GetBlogsByAuthor =\n    EF.CompileAsyncQuery((BlogContext context, int authorId) =>\n        context.Blogs.Where(b => b.AuthorId == authorId));\n\n// Utilisation\nvar blogs = await GetBlogsByAuthor(context, authorId).ToListAsync();\n```\n\n## Surveillance et profilage\n\n### 1. Activer la journalisation des requêtes\n\n```csharp\nprotected override void OnConfiguring(DbContextOptionsBuilder optionsBuilder)\n{\n    optionsBuilder\n        .UseSqlServer(connectionString)\n        .EnableSensitiveDataLogging()\n        .LogTo(Console.WriteLine, LogLevel.Information);\n}\n```\n\n### 2. Utiliser les étiquettes de requête\n\n```csharp\nvar blogs = context.Blogs\n    .TagWith(\"GetRecentBlogs\")\n    .Where(b => b.PublishedAt > DateTime.UtcNow.AddDays(-30))\n    .ToList();\n```\n\n### 3. Suivre les performances des requêtes\n\n```csharp\nvar stopwatch = Stopwatch.StartNew();\nvar blogs = await context.Blogs.ToListAsync();\nstopwatch.Stop();\n\n_logger.LogInformation(\"Requête exécutée en {ElapsedMilliseconds}ms\", stopwatch.ElapsedMilliseconds);\n```\n\n## Meilleures pratiques\n\n1. **Utilisez async/await** pour toutes les opérations de base de données\n2. **Évitez de charger des données inutiles** avec des projections\n3. **Utilisez des stratégies de chargement appropriées** (lazy, eager, explicit)\n4. **Implémentez une mise en cache appropriée** pour les données fréquemment consultées\n5. **Surveillez les plans d'exécution des requêtes** régulièrement\n6. **Utilisez efficacement le pooling de connexions**\n7. **Groupez les opérations** quand c'est possible\n8. **Gardez les contextes de courte durée** dans les applications web\n\n## Tests de performance\n\n```csharp\n[Fact]\npublic async Task GetRecentBlogs_ShouldExecuteInLessThan100ms()\n{\n    // Arrange\n    var stopwatch = Stopwatch.StartNew();\n    \n    // Act\n    var blogs = await _blogService.GetRecentBlogsAsync(10);\n    stopwatch.Stop();\n    \n    // Assert\n    Assert.True(stopwatch.ElapsedMilliseconds < 100);\n    Assert.Equal(10, blogs.Count);\n}\n```\n\nRappelez-vous : l'optimisation prématurée est la racine de tous les maux, mais être conscient de ces modèles vous aidera à écrire du code plus efficace dès le départ."),
